WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.738
The shop's apples arrive in a crate labeled apples.

2
00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:12.217
A helper needs a crate of fruit to carry to the front, and the apple crate seems fine: every apple is a fruit.

3
00:00:12.217 --> 00:00:16.812
But the helper was told the crate holds fruit, so they top it up with a lemon.

4
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.236
The next customer who reaches in for an apple bites into a lemon.

5
00:00:21.236 --> 00:00:22.605
Hold on to that face.

6
00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:24.039
It's the whole lesson.

7
00:00:24.933 --> 00:00:28.253
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:28.253 --> 00:00:29.608
An apple is a fruit.

9
00:00:29.608 --> 00:00:32.724
So why isn't a list of apples a list of fruit?

10
00:00:32.724 --> 00:00:36.992
Kotlin won't pass a MutableList&lt;Apple&gt; as a MutableList&lt;Fruit&gt;.

11
00:00:36.992 --> 00:00:39.431
Isn't the compiler just being fussy?

12
00:00:40.533 --> 00:00:45.239
To answer that, we need the test the compiler runs on every assignment.

13
00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:51.868
A type A is a subtype of B when an A can be used anywhere a B is expected, and nothing can go wrong.

14
00:00:51.868 --> 00:00:57.833
So ask: if I secretly hand over an A where a B is expected, can any allowed move go wrong?

15
00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.368
Try it on one apple.

16
00:00:59.368 --> 00:01:01.351
What can you do with a Fruit?

17
00:01:01.351 --> 00:01:02.308
Read its name.

18
00:01:02.308 --> 00:01:05.249
An apple has a name, so nothing goes wrong.

19
00:01:05.249 --> 00:01:07.164
Apple is a subtype of Fruit.

20
00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.482
Now run the same test on the crates.

21
00:01:10.482 --> 00:01:16.184
Someone expects a MutableList&lt;Fruit&gt;, and we secretly hand over a MutableList&lt;Apple&gt;.

22
00:01:16.184 --> 00:01:19.001
What moves are allowed on a list of fruit?

23
00:01:19.001 --> 00:01:21.886
Read an item: fine, every apple is a fruit.

24
00:01:21.886 --> 00:01:24.167
And add an item: any fruit at all.

25
00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:25.643
Can anything go wrong?

26
00:01:25.643 --> 00:01:26.716
Pause and guess.

27
00:01:30.467 --> 00:01:30.972
It can.

28
00:01:30.972 --> 00:01:33.713
Here it is in code, in both languages.

29
00:01:33.713 --> 00:01:36.093
Suppose the second line compiled.

30
00:01:36.093 --> 00:01:40.349
Then anyFruit and apples are two names for one list object.

31
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:42.933
One crate, with two labels.

32
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:46.574
Through the fruit label, adding a lemon is perfectly legal.

33
00:01:46.574 --> 00:01:50.771
Through the apples label, someone reads the second item as an apple.

34
00:01:50.771 --> 00:01:52.067
And that's the lemon.

35
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.432
So both compilers refuse the hand over itself.

36
00:01:56.432 --> 00:02:03.594
kotlinc says: initializer type mismatch, expected MutableList&lt;Fruit&gt;, actual MutableList&lt;Apple&gt;.

37
00:02:03.594 --> 00:02:07.996
javac says: List&lt;Apple&gt; cannot be converted to List&lt;Fruit&gt;.

38
00:02:08.133 --> 00:02:09.752
So the compiler isn't fussy.

39
00:02:09.752 --> 00:02:13.395
It's just the only one in the shop who thought about the lemon.

40
00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:18.177
Let's break it on purpose, to see what the rule saves us from.

41
00:02:18.177 --> 00:02:22.466
An unchecked cast forces the hand over, and the compiler only warns.

42
00:02:22.466 --> 00:02:25.935
Think of the warning as a shrug: fine, it's your lemon.

43
00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:30.456
The lesson's InvarianceDemo does exactly that, then adds a lemon.

44
00:02:30.456 --> 00:02:34.779
It prints: the apple crate holds 2 items, and nothing failed yet.

45
00:02:34.779 --> 00:02:38.569
Then a ClassCastException: Lemon cannot be cast to Apple.

46
00:02:38.733 --> 00:02:40.201
Notice where it failed.

47
00:02:40.201 --> 00:02:42.371
The lemon went in without a sound.

48
00:02:42.371 --> 00:02:46.455
The failure came later, on a read, on a line with no cast in it.

49
00:02:46.455 --> 00:02:51.560
It's the 12 in the box from lesson 1, with the compiler switched off on purpose.

50
00:02:52.467 --> 00:02:56.537
The crate picture breaks in one place, and it's the important one.

51
00:02:56.537 --> 00:03:02.089
A real crate holds its apples in plain sight, and you'd spot the lemon when you opened it.

52
00:03:02.089 --> 00:03:04.247
At run time, the JVM spots nothing.

53
00:03:04.247 --> 00:03:11.032
The list doesn't know it was meant for apples, so nothing checks the lemon until someone reads it as an apple.

54
00:03:11.933 --> 00:03:16.458
The lesson's title is the same story, with the widest types there are.

55
00:03:16.458 --> 00:03:19.302
Swap Apple for String, and Fruit for Object.

56
00:03:19.302 --> 00:03:23.375
Now the lemon is the number 42, dropped into a list of strings.

57
00:03:23.375 --> 00:03:27.512
That's why Java won't let a List&lt;String&gt; pass as a List&lt;Object&gt;.

58
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.303
Now the name.

59
00:03:29.303 --> 00:03:31.458
This rule is called invariance.

60
00:03:31.458 --> 00:03:37.156
MutableList&lt;A&gt; is a subtype of MutableList&lt;B&gt; only when A and B are the same type.

61
00:03:37.156 --> 00:03:42.576
The arrow from Apple to Fruit is real, but it doesn't carry over to the lists.

62
00:03:42.576 --> 00:03:45.564
Every Java generic type works the same way.

63
00:03:46.467 --> 00:03:48.936
In practice, invariance annoys people.

64
00:03:48.936 --> 00:03:55.239
You have a MutableList&lt;Apple&gt;, a function takes a MutableList&lt;Fruit&gt;, and the call won't compile.

65
00:03:55.239 --> 00:03:56.928
The fix is never the cast.

66
00:03:56.928 --> 00:03:59.592
Ask what the function does with the list.

67
00:03:59.733 --> 00:04:06.088
If it only reads, it should take a read-only List&lt;Fruit&gt;: a display case, with no way to put anything in.

68
00:04:06.088 --> 00:04:09.296
The end of this lesson shows why that one is allowed.

69
00:04:09.296 --> 00:04:15.711
And if it adds, the compiler just caught a bug: that function could have dropped a lemon into your apples.

70
00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.528
So, why isn't a crate of apples a crate of fruit?

71
00:04:19.528 --> 00:04:22.397
Because a crate of fruit lets you add any fruit.

72
00:04:22.397 --> 00:04:25.983
A list you can write to must match its element type exactly.

73
00:04:25.983 --> 00:04:27.656
Don't cast your way past it.

74
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:28.686
Quick check.

75
00:04:28.686 --> 00:04:30.900
crate is a MutableList&lt;Apple&gt;.

76
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.409
Which of these two lines compiles?

77
00:04:36.667 --> 00:04:37.159
Only b.

78
00:04:37.159 --> 00:04:42.085
A read-only List has no add, so reading apples as fruit can never lie.

79
00:04:42.085 --> 00:04:48.207
Kotlin declares it List&lt;out E&gt;, and the next lesson is about writing that out yourself.
